WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.079 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  when I was 19 I woke up one morning with  no feeling in the right half of my body  I just lost sensation in that part as if  a very thick layer of plastic was  covering my skin I pinched myself and I  scratched myself but I couldn't feel  anything that day I was preparing for my  first year finals in electrical  engineering but by the evening I  couldn't hold my pen after two weeks in  the hospital and endless examinations I  was diagnosed with multiple sclerosis I  was devastated I felt like my body was  betraying me and I was so terrified of  the uncertain future I walked around  weeks feeling so helpless but one  evening after crying the whole day I was  thinking about all the challenges that I  had overcome in my life and I was  reminded that in every situation I have  the freedom to choose it is true that I  have MS it is true that I cannot change  this fact but it is also true that I can  choose how I want to live my life and I  choose to live my life to the fullest as  if there is no MS I will take the daily  treatment but I will not give up on  myself I will study work hard travel go  out chase my dreams with no excuses  everything as before and even more  because that moment that I realized that  I have the freedom to choose changed my  life forever it turned the MS for my  biggest enemy to a gift because now I  value every moment I was born in Israel  to an Arab Bedouin family mad winds are  nomadic Arabs they traditionally lived  in tribes it's a very conservative  society  constitute 3% of the Israeli population  my father lived with two wives which is  a common practice in the Bedouin culture  but shortly after their marriage my  mother decided to flee the village she  did not want to raise her children in a  place where women are inferior and get  killed for reasons of what is called  honour honor killings horrified my  mother so she fled my father's house and  she moved in a hariya a Jewish town in  northern Israel there she raised me and  my eight siblings on her own we were the  only non Jewish family in town and I  found myself on the border between two  cultures the Jewish and Arab in school  my siblings and I suffered from racism  until one day the social worker decided  that we did not fit into the Jewish  society and decided to send us to a  boarding school in an Arab village at  the age of six I was separated from my  mother I spent three years in the  boarding school suffering from hunger  abuse and bullying this time from the  people of the village who were biased  against the kids from the boarding  school they considered us bad influence  they didn't want us to sit next to their  kids in class or even passed by their  home I literally had nothing in life  poor and wanted and misfit in both  societies and treated as inferior with  no father to protect and a mother who  was far away even when she sent us  birthday gifts they would be taken away  but one thing that I had one thing that  no one could take away from me with my  thoughts and hopes for a better future I  realized that I was born to this reality  but I could not change it but  could change my future and this hope is  what God kept me going and working to be  the first in my class even when your  choices are limited you can always  choose your thoughts and then this  happened at the age of 14 I was  discovered by a fashion photographer and  I became the first-ever Israeli Bedouin  fashion model landing large campaigns  with companies such as diesel and huge  amount of national exposure the phone in  our house didn't stop ringing some were  calling to encourage some were calling  to check if I'm still alive and some  were calling to say that soon I would  not be I was shunned by the Arab society  and my uncle threatened to cut off my  head if I did not stop bringing dishonor  to our family these were hard times for  me but I was again reminded of the  painful and brave choices my mother had  made for us when she fled the village  and like her I decided to live by my own  values not the values of my uncle not  the values of anyone else but by my own  values and with my mother by my side I  found the courage to push back and send  a message to all the Arab girls telling  them that we can pursue any path we  choose and cannot be stopped by the  chains of an over conservative society  and what I learned from this experience  is if you wish to live your life to the  fullest  you must choose your own values and live  your life accordingly never live by the  values of others when I was 18 I decided  to volunteer for the Israeli Defense  Forces so I applied for the student  soldier program in electrical  engineering and I was rejected I was  told that Arab  are not allowed to participate in  research and development in the military  and I should pick a lesser role this was  painful like hitting a wall but I wasn't  willing to let the IDF's tell me how I  could contribute to my country or what I  could or could not do so I pursued the  matter aggressively I sent letters to  the head of HR I called in favors and  get as many people involved as I could  all the commanders I reached out to told  me that this is a dead end there is  nothing you can do about it and you  better stop wasting your time but I  insisted and after a year-long struggle  I became the first Bedouin in the  Israeli history to enroll to research  and development students soldier program  I was over the moon I knew that I had  won and what I learned from this  experience is never let others tell you  what you can or cannot do never let them  limit or eliminate your choices you know  yourself best and you have the freedom  to choose your actions you have the  freedom to stretch beyond the boundaries  that other said for you  if I listen to anyone who told me you  can't I wouldn't be standing here in  front of you at Stanford when I was  thinking about the message that I wanted  to deliver in this talk I wasn't  thinking about you my Stanford and VA  community I was thinking about girls and  villages like the one I grew up in who  are constantly judged and chained to the  values of an over conservative society I  was thinking about kids from  disadvantaged backgrounds who have all  the powers working against them but  challenges are bound to be in life even  if you made it here to Stanford my  multiple sclerosis  it's not because of my humble beginnings  or because I'm a woman from a  conservative society it is a just life  when life brings challenges when you  feel that life is closing in on you and  you have no choices remember you have  your you always have your thoughts you  always have your values you always have  your actions you have the power to  choose  [Applause]  [Music]

